{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-130003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x})-x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x)(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}-(x)^{′}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1)x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-130003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-77674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{sin}^{2}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-77674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x+4({sin}^{2}⁡x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x=2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{sin}^{2}⁡x$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-77674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-77674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-77674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-77674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-90699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(4{x}^{3}-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2})-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=(2x-2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+(4{x}^{3}-4{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution while ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-130005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-t}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t}+2(-{e}^{-t})+{e}^{-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-t}+2(-{e}^{1-t})+{e}^{1-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-t}{({e}^{1-t})}^{′}-{({e}^{-t})}^{′}{e}^{1-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(-{e}^{1-t})-(-{e}^{-t}){e}^{1-t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-79930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-90911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B"] ["math" "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x"] ["math" "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x"] ["math" "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x"] ["math" "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-90911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{-2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t})+5({u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t})+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-10u{e}^{-2t}+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}+{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}({u}^{″}+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{-2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}={e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{e}^{-5t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-129956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-5t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{3t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t})+9(u{e}^{3t}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}-18u{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{3t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{3t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}\\cdot t=t{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{6t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-65120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{2t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-129942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-129942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4})+8({u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x})+16(u{e}^{-4x}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-32u{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{-4x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}\\cdot x=x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{-8x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-79931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{8x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-15y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)+x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+12{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+12u{x}^{3}+{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+3u{x}^{3}-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+13{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2{w}^{′}x+13w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-11/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{11}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-130040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{4/3}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{7/2}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{x}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-6t{y}^{′}+12y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{3}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-6t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+12(u{t}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-6{u}^{′}t-18u+12u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{5}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{5}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{6}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-65123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{t}^{3}+Bt"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-65123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-4t{y}^{′}+6y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-4t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-8u{t}^{2}+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{2}={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-130017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{\\sqrt{t}}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-2t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+2(u{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-4u{t}^{2}+2u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}(t{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nt{w}^{′}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{t}^{2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+Bt.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-79934" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79934" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\frac{1}{t}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{-2}"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-79934" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ is a solution to the equation $2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{-1}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3})+3t({u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2})-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-4{u}^{′}+4u{t}^{-1}+3{u}^{′}-3u{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2t{w}^{′}-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{-1}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}){t}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3/2}\\cdot {t}^{-1}={t}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-130012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{A}{t}+Bt"] ["math" "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B{t}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-130012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=6{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{3x})^{′}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{e}^{3x})^{′}=9{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(9{e}^{3x})+(3{e}^{3x})-6({e}^{3x})=6{e}^{3x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{3x})^{′}={e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})^{′}=6{e}^{3x}+9x{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(6{e}^{3x}+9x{e}^{3x})+({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-6(x{e}^{3x})=6{e}^{3x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981405d09c8211b6"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [[