"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{2x}+B"] ["math" "A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Cx{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "C{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$"] ["text" "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339878" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $B$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+10y=4x\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-6x(1)+10(x)=4x.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=4x.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a92ae8c3e61d85407dc3ab5f525e8bb61ae804a9a300402e981