re-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Cx{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "C{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x{e}^{-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$"] ["text" "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "q-339876" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $B$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/state.json" "file_sha256" "5394be542298dab6097c7663ece3dabbb9fc1a3e49ed07169af34705f8f48a67"} {"question" "e-6792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-6792.json" "file_sha256" "f7a70d29842183009b64e8577308fc17723d3373c46ece75a4dffefd1b598f42"} {"question" "e-6792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-6792.json" "file_sha256" "f7a70d29842183009b64e8577308fc17723d3373c46ece75a4dffefd1b598f42"} {"question" "e-6789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-6789.json" "file_sha256" "2ec343689745bb3ce9878f28d3c133918bf55d00f3db64d206914bacecccd150"} {"question" "e-6789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}={e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-6789.json" "file_sha256" "2ec343689745bb3ce9878f28d3c133918bf55d00f3db64d206914bacecccd150"} {"question" "e-6794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-6794.json" "file_sha256" "695d7cd948b251f2bac6b593442b3cd901a631b4681cbf3d441ffdf5671a8f30"} {"question" "e-6794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-6794.json" "file_sha256" "695d7cd948b251f2bac6b593442b3cd901a631b4681cbf3d441ffdf5671a8f30"} {"question" "e-21879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-21879.json" "file_sha256" "6c3bc9424e7f90afd04f3fef8eb14da5bee9870d63bb4c3a8562e64eadbff158"} {"question" "e-21879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{6}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063363/example-21879.json" "file_sha256" "6c3bc9424e7f90afd04f3fef8eb14da5bee9870d63bb4c3a8562e64eadbff158"}] "usage" {"e-6792" [] "e-6794" [] "q-339917" [] "e-21879" [] "e-6789" []} "basis" 2043 "retractions" [[17592186152495 :question/problem "$x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} =-6x^{2}$\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ax^{5} + B$, where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\nThe function $y_{p}(x) = x$\n{{field-1}}\na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nThe function $y_{p}(x) = x^{2}$\n{{field-2}}\na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\nwhere\n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants."] [17592186152495 :question/answer-fields 17592186149865] [17592186151911 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$."] [17592186151911 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n$y_{1}^{′}(x)|= e^{x}; y_{1}^{″}(x)|= e^{x}$,\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n$e^{x} - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark$\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n$y_{2}^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x}; y_{2}^{″}(x)|= 4e^{2x}$,\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n$4e^{2x} - 3(2e^{2x}) + 2(e^{2x}) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark$\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n$W(y_{1}, y_{2})|=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}; = y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2}; = e^{x}(e^{2x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}e^{2x}; = 2e^{3x} - e^{3x}; = e^{3x}$.\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0$, the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III."] [17592186151909 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution."] [17592186151909 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\ny_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} = e^{5x}(u^{′} + 5u) \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= e^{5x}(u^{″} + 10u^{′} + 25u)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{5x}(u^{″} + 10u^{′} + 25u) - 10e^{5x}(u^{′} + 5u) + 25e^{5x}u &= 0 \\\\\ne^{5x}(u^{″} + 10u^{′} + 25u - 10u^{′} - 50u + 25u) &= 0 \\\\\ne^{5x}(u^{″}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^{5x}\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = ue^{5x}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right)\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}\n$$\n\nFinally, because\n\n$$\nW(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}\n$$"] [17592186151912 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution."] [17592186151912 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}(x) &= u(x)y_{1}(x) \\\\\n&= u(x) \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= (u(x))/(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating\n\n$$\ny_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= \\frac{u}{x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= \\frac{u^{′}x - u}{x^{2}} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= \\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} + 3xy^{′} + y &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}(\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u}{x^{3}}) + 3x(\\frac{u^{′}x - u}{x^{2}}) + \\frac{u}{x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u + 3u^{′}x - 3u + u}{x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} + u^{′}x}{x} &= 0 \\\\\nu^{″}x + u^{′} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'$. Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}\n$$\n\nSince $w = u'$, we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nFinally, because\n\n$$\nW(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}\n$$"] [17592186151837 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2$, where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n$x^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3$\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution."] [17592186151837 :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x$, we have\n$y^{′}(x)|= (x)^{′} = 1,; y^{″}(x)|= (1)^{′} = 0$.\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n$x^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}$\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3$, we have\n$y^{′}(x)|= (x^{3})^{′} = 3x^{2},; y^{″}(x)|= (3x^{2})^{′} = 6x$.\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n$x^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= Ax^{6} + Bx^{2} + x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186149866 17592186149867 17592186149868 17592186149869 17592186149871 17592186149872 17592186149873 17592186149874 17592186149876 17592186149877 17592186149879 17592186149880 17592186149881 17592186149882 17592186149884 17592186149885] "immutable_fields" [[17592186149865 :answer-field/id] [17592186149865 :answer-field/key] [17592186149865 :answer-field/type] [17592186149870 :answer-field/id] [17592186149870 :answer-field/key] [17592186149870 :answer-field/type] [17592186149875 :answer-f